  'The first congregation formed in Franklin County, in 1807. Its first church on this site, of stone, went up in 1826; a brick church replaced it in 1852, and the present building, the third, in 1883. The 1852 church is thought to have had passages for people escaping slavery on their way to Canada, and one such tunnel survives in the basement of the present church. Vice President William A. Wheeler was a member.',
